fresh output dir `dc-warn/out/w1-policy/evidence/gen-w1/`.

| step | result | exit code (measured directly, never through a pipe) |
|---|---|---|
| generation | rc=0, stderr 0 bytes (`evidence/gen-w1.stderr`) | **0** |
| `swiftc -typecheck` over `gen-w1/gap/Gap.swift gen-w1/Runtime.swift gen-w1/std/UStringException.swift gen-w1/std/UStringFault.swift` | rc=0, **0 errors, 2 warnings** (`evidence/swiftc-w1.stderr`) | **0** |

The two diagnostics, verbatim:

```
.../gen-w1/gap/Gap.swift:113:17: warning: result of 'ReadOnlyArray<Element>' initializer is unused [#no-usage]
.../gen-w1/gap/Gap.swift:115:17: warning: result of 'ReadOnlyArray<Element>' initializer is unused [#no-usage]
```

Regenerated `Gap.swift` sha256 `2be5e102d695146439d3fdab3c1aeee7f8096d7d1460b92fd9535055c70c02ab` —
identical to the hash pinned in FREEZE.md §5.2, so I reproduced the exact same output.

The defect shape, confirmed by reading my regenerated file (lines 110–118):

```swift
public static func switchExplicitReturn(_ choice: GapChoice) -> ReadOnlyArray<Int32> {
    switch choice {
        case .one:
            ReadOnlyArray(Gap.sourceFirst())     // line 113 — value discarded
        case .two:
            ReadOnlyArray(Gap.sourceSecond())    // line 115 — value discarded
    }
    return ReadOnlyArray(Gap.sourceFirst())      // line 117 — unconditional
}
```

Both switch arms evaluate and discard their value; the function unconditionally returns
`sourceFirst()`. `switchExplicitReturn(.two)` therefore returns the **wrong value**. The two
warnings are exactly the W1 swallowed-return shape. This matches the archive's
`expected-outcomes.md` §W1 and the mechanism note there (`armLines` pre-conversion drops the
`return` keyword, `SwiftExpr.hx:5639-5642` region).

